## Why This Is Asked
Tests tail latency, backpressure, and autoscaling in an asynchronous, event-driven path, mirroring real‑world ride‑hailing workloads.

## Key Concepts
- Tail latency under backpressure
- Async pipelines with Kafka
- Backpressure signals (lag, queue depth)
- Autoscaling (HPA, KEDA) and circuit breakers
- Realistic workload mix and failure scenarios

## Code Example

```javascript
import http from 'k6/http';
import { sleep } from 'k6';
export let options = { stages: [ { duration: '10m', target: 200 }, { duration: '20m', target: 800 } ] };
export default function () {
  http.post('https://api.example.com/v1/rides', JSON.stringify({ pickup: 'A', dropoff: 'B' }), { headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' } });
  sleep(Math.random() * 0.5);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you verify SLA breach containment? what alerts and runbooks?
- How would you isolate bottlenecks across API, Kafka, and workers in chaos runs?

## Why This Is Asked

Assess end-to-end latency across a fan-out path, backpressure resilience, region-aware autoscaling, and observability for a large-scale chat system with moderation integration.

## Key Concepts

- End-to-end latency and tail behavior (P99/P99.9)
- Backpressure and queue depth management
- Region-aware autoscaling and load distribution
- Observability: tracing, metrics, dashboards, and alerting
- Fault injection: moderation outages, latency spikes, network partitions

## Code Example

```javascript
// P99 latency measurement sketch
function onMessageSent(event) {
  const t0 = performance.now();
  deliverToRecipients(event);
  const latency = performance.now() - t0;
  recordMetric('e2e_latency_ms', latency);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you model and test regional failover guarantees?
- What telemetry would you add to differentiate moderation latency from delivery latency?

## Why This Is Asked
Real-time chat imposes tight latency budgets and backpressure handling under bursty activity and regional split. This probes workload modeling, autoscaling behavior, and resilience.

## Key Concepts
- WebSocket throughput and latency budgets
- Backpressure and queueing behavior
- Regional load testing and latency variance
- Autoscaling thresholds and warm-up times
- Resilience testing with failover

## Code Example
```javascript
// k6 script skeleton to establish WebSocket connections and message bursts
import ws from 'k6/ws';
import { check, sleep } from 'k6';
export default function () {
  const url = 'wss://chat.example.com/ws';
  const res = ws.connect(url, {}, function (socket) {
    socket.on('open', function () { socket.send(JSON.stringify({type:'join', room:'room1'})); });
    socket.on('message', function (data) { /* handle */ });
    for (let i=0; i<100; i++) { socket.send(JSON.stringify({type:'message', text:'hi'})); }
  });
  check(res, { 'connected': (r)=> r && r.status === 101 });
  sleep(1);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you measure and isolate network variance impact on latency across regions?
- What autoscaling strategy would you choose for WS brokers and message queues under bursty traffic?

